{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-golden-skew-dual-node-interpretation","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:golden-skew-dual-node-interpretation","kind":"remark","title":"Mathematical novelty of the skew-dual stability margin","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-golden-skew-dual-node"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-golden-skew-dual-node","text":"golden-skew-dual-node-interpretation.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:golden-skew-dual-node-interpretation\"></a><p><strong>Remark (Stability margin).</strong> The reciprocal node bar-duality (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varphi^{-2}</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varphi^2</code>) functions as a two-sided stability margin. This margin provides a boundary condition for bounding spectral gaps.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/predicted-spectra/golden-skew-dual-node-interpretation.md","heading":"Remark","number":"15.18.7"}
